# Documentation voice and SEO (ECP)

## Voice

- **Technically friendly, light**: precise terms, low-jargon sentences, minimal hype.
- **Forward-moving**: prefer “what this is / why it matters / how to use it next.”
- **Helpful, not spammy**: avoid buzzword piles, “ultimate guide” phrasing, and repetitive keyword loops.
- **Clarity over cleverness**: simple headings, short paragraphs, concrete examples.
- **Active voice + second person**: “you can…” / “run…” / “define…”.
- **No emojis**: avoid emojis in headings, body text, tables, and UI callouts.

## Terminology and naming

- **First mention on a page**: write **“ECP (Execution Control Protocol)”**.
- **After that**: alternate naturally between **ECP**, **Execution Control Protocol**, and **the protocol** when context is clear.
- **Prefer**: Workflow, Environment, Ecp, workflow files, plugin, journal.
- **Avoid**: presenting YAML Context / `ECPContext` / `spec.yaml` as current.
- **Avoid**: harnesses, browser demo, Chrome AI, EQL, `ecp test`, `ecp serve`, `ecp up`, root `ecp encode` / `ecp decode`.
- **Avoid**: repeating “Execution Control Protocol” in every paragraph; it should read like normal technical writing.

## SEO that still reads well

- **Intent-first intro**: first 2–3 sentences should answer what the page is for and who it helps.
- **Headings**: sentence case; use clear, specific H2s that match real queries (no clickbait).
- **Internal links**: add 2–5 relevant links to deeper docs (concepts, workflow files, security, MCP relationship, CLI) where helpful.
- **Comparisons**: state what ECP is and isn’t; use crisp “X is for…, ECP is for…” framing.
- **Commands**: prefer installed CLIs over one-off runners in docs. Show the one-time install/link step, then the command. Intended install is `npm install -g @executioncontrolprotocol/cli@next`; until `@next` exists, link [build from source](/getting-started/build-from-source).
- **Public-only docs**: do not publish internal documentation about building or operating the docs site (Mintlify, `mint dev`, `docs.json`, contribution workflow). Pages should focus on installing, configuring, and running ECP.
- **Do not leak internal ops**: never document analytics backends, prompt/telemetry logging vendors or pipelines, private env var catalogs, feature flags, or unpublished roadmap/UI details on public MDX or in `.mintlify/skills/`.
- **MCP / App**: placeholder pages only until Phases 7 and 8. Do not document a hosted browser demo.

## Content checks before publishing

- Title + description include **ECP** and **Execution Control Protocol** at least once (naturally).
- H1 is unique and matches the title’s intent.
- A “Next steps” or “Related reading” section exists when appropriate.
- No internal vendor/ops names or private env setup unless they are part of the public product API users must configure themselves (e.g. `secrets("key")`).
